.TH PROFILELAB 2
.SH NAME
profilelab: run, parse, march \- Danby radial structure models as scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "draw.m";
include "profilelab.m";
profilelab := load Profilelab Profilelab->PATH;

err := profilelab->run(ctxt, name, spec);
(m, start, span, samples, normalised, err) := profilelab->parse(spec);
data := profilelab->march(m, start, span, samples, normalised);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Profilelab
is the last of the seven Danby viewer families as a library the model
calls:
.PP
.EX
profilelab->run(ctxt, "whitedwarf", SPEC);
.EE
.PP
This family is not an evolution in time.  A model integrates OUTWARD from
the centre of a star, and what is plotted is the profile the march leaves
behind, so the independent variable is a radius and the graph's x axis is
that radius rather than a clock.  The symbol
.B r
is bound to the current radius, the way
.B t
is bound to time in the other families.
.PP
The vocabulary is
.IR dynsys (2)'s
\(em
.BR param ,
.BR state ,
.BR derivative ,
.BR eq ,
.B observable
\(em plus
.BR title ,
.B manual
and three of profilelab's own:
.TP
.BI radius " startexpr " , " spanexpr"
Where the march begins and how far it goes.  Both are expressions over
the parameters, because in this family they are usually parameters
themselves.  The step is
.IR span /( samples \-1),
so the march ends a little beyond
.IR start + span ;
that is what these models have always done, and the outermost sample is
the one the normalisation divides by.
.TP
.BI samples " n"
How many points.  320 if not said.
.TP
.BI normalise " name"
Divide that observable by its own value at the outermost sample, which is
how an enclosed mass becomes a fraction of the total without knowing the
total in advance.
.PP
Exactly two
.B observable
lines are required; they are the two curves.  There is no time here, so
there is no
.B maxtime
line \(em
.IR dynsys (2)
wants one and
.I profilelab
supplies it.
.PP
The march is explicit Euler, not the Runge-Kutta the time-stepping
families use, because that is what these models are: the profile IS the
sequence of Euler steps, and integrating it more accurately would draw a
different curve from the one the model describes.  Both states are
clamped at zero after every step, as this family always has.
.B March
runs it and returns
.IR samples *3
reals \(em radius, then the two observables \(em with the normalisation
applied;
.IR profilelabtest (1)
uses it without a display.
.PP
The window is Tk's.  The parameter sliders are Tk scales, the button is a
Tk button, the legend is a Tk label, and the layout is a Tk geometry
manager's; the graph is a
.IR panel (9)
widget holding an image the library draws into.  Nothing runs on a timer
here: a profile is computed when a parameter changes and then sits there,
so it is redrawn on an event rather than forty times a second.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/profilelab.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR profilelabtest (1),
.IR flightlab (2),
.IR poplab (2),
.IR dynsys (2)
.SH BUGS
Where a density reaches zero the original programs divided a pressure by
it.  Limbo's own real division answered 0/0 with zero, so those models
quietly kept a flat zero density outside the star;
.IR hoc (2)
raises on a zero denominator instead, so the specs guard the division
with
.B step
and get the same zero deliberately.  The numbers agree exactly, but a
spec written without that guard will raise where the C-shaped original
would have limped on.
